{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassDerivation","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassDerivation","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt","lean:module:QOU.Glossary","lean:module:QOU.KnotTheory","lean:module:QOU.Torsion","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":24,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.mass_ratio_from_volume","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.mass_ratio_from_volume","kind":"theorem","line":298,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L298-L310","doc":"The quantum Planck constant squared determines the mass ratio. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.derive_substrate_q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.derive_substrate_q","kind":"theorem","line":211,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L211-L296","doc":"**Theorem**: Given the topological mass conjecture and experimental data,\nthe substrate parameter `q` must be approximately 1.1097.\n\nDerivation steps:\n1. From `m_μ/m_e = Vol(4₁) / ℏ_q²`, solve for `ℏ_q²`\n2. `ℏ_q² = Vol(4₁) / (m_μ/m_e) ≈ 2.02988 / 206.768 ≈ 0.009817`\n3. `ℏ_q ≈ 0.09908`\n4. From `ℏ_q = 1 - 1/q`, solve: `1/q = 1 - 0.09908 = 0.90092`\n5. `q ≈ 1.1097` ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.planck_sq_from_mass_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.planck_sq_from_mass_eq","kind":"theorem","line":191,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L191-L209","doc":"The mass equation pins down `(1 - 1/q)²` to a specific rational value. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.quantum_planck_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.quantum_planck_inv","kind":"theorem","line":184,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L184-L189","doc":"Algebraic identity: from the mass equation we can express `q` in terms\nof `h_q = quantum_planck q = 1 - 1/q`, yielding `q = 1 / (1 - h_q)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.quantum_planck_approx","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.quantum_planck_approx","kind":"theorem","line":174,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L174-L180","doc":"The quantum Planck constant for q ≈ 1.1097 is approximately 0.09886.\n(The exact value is 1097/11097 ≈ 0.098856; the error vs 0.09908 is ~2.3e-4.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.mu_e_ratio_approx","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.mu_e_ratio_approx","kind":"theorem","line":167,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L167-L172","doc":"The muon-electron mass ratio is approximately 206.7683. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.sqrt_substrate_q_bracket","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.sqrt_substrate_q_bracket","kind":"theorem","line":161,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L161-L165","doc":"Two-sided bracket on `Real.sqrt substrate_q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.sqrt_substrate_q_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.sqrt_substrate_q_lt","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L149-L159","doc":"`Real.sqrt substrate_q < 1.05343`. Proof: `1.05343² ≈\n1.1097148 > 1.1097`, so by monotonicity of `Real.sqrt`.\n(The previous bound `1.05342` was false: `1.05342² ≈ 1.1096937\n< 1.1097`, i.e. `√substrate_q ≈ 1.0534228` exceeds it.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.sqrt_substrate_q_gt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.sqrt_substrate_q_gt","kind":"theorem","line":140,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L140-L147","doc":"`Real.sqrt substrate_q > 1.05341`. Proof: `1.05341² ≈\n1.10967 < 1.1097`, so by monotonicity of `Real.sqrt`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.vol_figure_eight_approx","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.vol_figure_eight_approx","kind":"theorem","line":126,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L126-L131","doc":"The figure-eight volume is approximately 2.029883. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.substrate_q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.substrate_q","kind":"def","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L121-L122","doc":"The substrate coupling constant (derived value). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.quantum_planck","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.quantum_planck","kind":"def","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L117-L119","doc":"The quantum Planck constant derived from substrate parameter q.\n`ℏ_q = 1 - 1/q` for `q > 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.experimental_mu_e_ratio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.experimental_mu_e_ratio","kind":"def","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L112-L115","doc":"CODATA 2022 Muon-Electron mass ratio.\nNIST: 206.768 2827(46), rel. uncertainty 2.2 × 10⁻⁸.\nRef: https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?mmusme ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.vol_figure_eight","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.vol_figure_eight","kind":"def","line":99,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L99-L110","doc":"The Hyperbolic Volume of the Figure-Eight Knot (4₁).\nExact: `2 · Cl₂(π/3) = 6 · Λ(π/3)`, where `Cl₂` is the Clausen function\nand `Λ` the Lobachevsky function, `Λ(θ) = Cl₂(2θ)/2` (OEIS A091518).\nThe complement decomposes into two regular ideal tetrahedra, each of\nvolume `Cl₂(π/3) = 3 · Λ(π/3) ≈ 1.01494`.\nComputed by SnapPy; value from Bloch-Wigner dilogarithm. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.topological_mass_conjecture","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.topological_mass_conjecture","kind":"theorem","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L91-L95","doc":"Backward-compatible wrapper: given a `TopologicalMassFormula`,\nthe mass equation holds for its specific values. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.topological_mass_formula","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.topological_mass_formula","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L72-L89","doc":"The topological mass formula, **derived** from its defining data.\n\nGiven a positive knot volume `vol`, a non-zero quantum Planck\nconstant `h_q`, and the holonomy relation `mass · h_q² = vol`\n(the nonabelian multiplicative integral over the exceptional\ndivisors), the mass is `mass = Vol(K) / ℏ_q²`.\n\nThis is a genuine algebraic derivation (clearing `h_q² ≠ 0` from\nthe holonomy relation), not a verbatim restatement of a structure\nfield: the input is the *product* form `mass · h_q² = vol`\nproduced by the multiplicative integral, and the quotient form is\nobtained by `eq_div_of_mul_eq`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula.mass_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula.mass_eq","kind":"theorem","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L70-L70","doc":"**The mass formula**: `m = Vol(K) / ℏ_q²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula.vol_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula.vol_pos","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L68-L68","doc":"The volume is positive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula.h_q_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula.h_q_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L66-L66","doc":"The quantum Planck constant is non-zero. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula.h_q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula.h_q","kind":"def","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L64-L64","doc":"The quantum Planck constant `ℏ_q = 1 - 1/q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula.vol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula.vol","kind":"def","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L62-L62","doc":"The hyperbolic volume of the knot `K`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula.mass","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula.mass","kind":"def","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L60-L60","doc":"The mass of the particle. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula.mk","kind":"ctor","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L58-L58","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassDerivation.TopologicalMassFormula","kind":"structure","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassDerivation.lean#L44-L70","doc":"**Theorem 4.34** (Topological Mass Formula).\nFor a particle identified with knot `K` whose vortex topology is\ngenerated by blow-ups, the mass satisfies `m = Vol(K) / ℏ_q²`\nwhere `ℏ_q = 1 - 1/q` is the quantum Planck constant and `Vol(K)`\narises from evaluating the nonabelian multiplicative integral over\nthe exceptional divisors of the knot's blow-up sequence.\n\nThis is a consequence of Theorem `topological_mass_decomposition`\n(Torsion.lean): the baseline holonomy is `Vol(K)` and the\nexceptional contributions sum to the quantum dilogarithm terms\n`Φ_q(zᵢ)`, yielding `m = Vol(K) / ℏ_q²` after evaluation in the\nspectral coordinates of the Briggs–Klein chart.\n\nLaTeX: `\\label{thm:topological-mass}` ","signatureChunk":0}]}